<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<subsection xml:id="law_of_cosines" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
        <title>The Law of Cosines</title>
        <p>
                        There is another important identity for general triangles called the <em>law of cosines</em>. It is simple to state, but it is a bit harder to derive, so we will state it first, set up the machinery we need to derive it, and then go through the whole derivation.
        </p>
        <theorem xml:id='thm_law_of_cosines'>
                <title>The Law of Cosines</title>
                <statement>
                        <p>
                                        For any triangle with angles
                                <m>A</m>,
                                <m>B</m>,
                                and
                                <m>C</m>,
                                and with side lengths opposite each angle given by 
                                <m>a</m>,
                                <m>b</m>,
                                and
                                <m>c</m>, respectively,
                        <md number="yes" xml:id='eq_law_of_cosines'>
                                c^{2}=a^{2}+b^{2}-2ab\cos(C).
                        </md>
                        </p>
                </statement>
        <proof>
                <p>
                                Let <m>\triangle{}ABC</m> be an arbitrary triangle with vertices <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m>, and opposite side lengths <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>, respectively. We construct the altitude (perpendicular segment) from <m>A</m> to the point <m>H</m> on the line determined by <m>\overline{BC}</m>. We need to deal with two different cases. In one case, we have an interior altitude, as shown in <xref ref='fig_interior_alt'/>, and in the other case, we have an exterior altitude, as shown in <xref ref='fig_exterior_alt'/>.
                </p>
        <figure xml:id='fig_interior_alt'>
                <caption>A Triangle with an Interior Altitude</caption>
                <image width="50%">
                        <description>
                                <p>
                                        An arbitrary triangle with an interior altitude.
                                                </p>
                                        <p>
                                                The vertices are A B and C, with C on the
                                        far left, B on the far right, horizontally even with C, and A
                                        above and between C and B.
                                </p>
                                <p>
                                        The sides opposite each angle are labeled a, b, and c
                                        respectively.
                                </p>
                                <p>
                                        A perpendicular dashed line, labeled h, descends from A to point H on segment BC.
                                </p>
                                <p>
                                        Segment C H is labeled x and segment B H is labeled a-x.
                                </p>
                        </description>
                        <xi:include href="./law_of_cosines_image.xml" />
                </image>
        </figure>
                <p>
                        Referring to <xref ref='fig_interior_alt'/>, we see that we have right triangles <m>\triangle{}CHA</m> and <m>\triangle{}BHA</m>. Let <m>h</m> be the length of the altitude <m>\overline{AH}</m>, let <m>x</m> be the length of segment <m>\overline{CH}</m>, and thus the length of segment <m>\overline{HB}</m> will be <m>(a-x)</m>. From the Pythagorean theorem, we have 
                        <md>
                                <mrow>
                                        c^{2}
                                                &amp;=
                                        h^{2}+(a-x)^{2}
                                        &amp;
                                        \text{and}
                                        &amp;
                                        &amp;
                                        b^{2}
                                                &amp;=
                                        h^{2}+x^{2}.
                                </mrow>
                        </md>
                        Also, from the right triangle interpretation of cosine, we have
                        <md>
                                <mrow>
                                \cos\left(C\right)=\frac{x}{b}.
                                </mrow>
                        </md>
                                Because <m>b^{2}=h^{2}+x^{2}</m>, we have <m>h^{2}=b^{2}-x^{2}</m>, and because <m>\cos\left(C\right)=x/b</m>, we have <m>x=b\cos\left(C\right)</m>. Thus, we have
                                <md>
                                        <mrow>
                                                c^{2}&amp;=h^{2}+\left(a-x\right)^{2}
                                        </mrow>
                                        <mrow>
                                                     &amp;=h^{2}+\left(a^{2}-2ax+x^{2}\right)
                                        </mrow>
                                        <mrow>
                                                     &amp;=\left(b^{2}-x^{2}\right)+\left(a^{2}-2ax+x^{2}\right)
                                        </mrow>
                                        <mrow>
                                                     &amp;=b^{2}-x^{2}+a^{2}-2ax+x^{2}
                                        </mrow>
                                        <mrow>
                                                     &amp;=b^{2}+a^{2}-2ax
                                        </mrow>
                                        <mrow>
                                                     &amp;=b^{2}+a^{2}-2a\left(b\cos\left(C\right)\right)
                                        </mrow>
                                        <mrow>
                                                     &amp;=b^{2}+a^{2}-2ab\cos\left(C\right),
                                        </mrow>
                                </md>
                                as desired.
                </p>
                <p>
                        In the case of an exterior altitude, as shown in <xref ref="fig_exterior_alt"/>, we again have right triangles <m>\triangle{}CHA</m> and <m>\triangle{}BHA</m>, and we let <m>h</m> be the length of the altitude <m>\overline{AH}</m> and let <m>x</m> be the length of segment <m>\overline{CH}</m>. The length of segment <m>\overline{HB}</m> then will be <m>(x-a)</m>. From the Pythagorean theorem, we have 
                        <md>
                                <mrow>
                                        c^{2}
                                                &amp;=
                                        h^{2}+(x-a)^{2}
                                        &amp;
                                        \text{and}
                                        &amp;
                                        &amp;
                                        b^{2}
                                                &amp;=
                                        h^{2}+x^{2}.
                                </mrow>
                        </md>
                        And from the right triangle interpretation of cosine, we have
                        <md>
                                <mrow>
                                \cos\left(C\right)=\frac{x}{b}.
                                </mrow>
                        </md>
                        The only difference between this case and the interior altitude case is that we have <m>(x-a)^{2}</m> instead of <m>(a-x)^{2}</m>. However,
                        <md>
                                <mrow>
                                (x-a)^{2}
                                &amp;=
                                (x-a)(x-a)
                                </mrow>
                                <mrow>
                                &amp;=
                                (-1)\cdot(-1)(x-a)(x-a)
                                </mrow>
                                <mrow>
                                &amp;=
                                (-1)\cdot(x-a)(-1)\cdot(x-a)
                                </mrow>
                                <mrow>
                                &amp;=
                                (-x+a)(-x-a)
                                </mrow>
                                <mrow>
                                &amp;=
                                (a-x)(a-x)
                                </mrow>
                                <mrow>
                                &amp;=
                                        (a-x)^{2}
                                </mrow>
                        </md>
                        and so the rest of the proof is the same.
                </p>
        </proof>
        </theorem>
        <figure xml:id='fig_exterior_alt'>
                <caption>A Triangle with an Exterior Altitude</caption>
                <image width="50%">
                        <description>
                                <p>
                                        An arbitrary triangle with an exterior altitude.
                                                </p>
                                        <p>
                                                The vertices are A B and C, with A on the
                                        far left, B on the far right, horizontally even with A, and C
                                        above and to the right of B.
                                </p>
                                <p>
                                        The sides opposite each angle are labeled a, b, and c
                                        respectively.
                                </p>
                                <p>
                                        A dashed line extends segment A B to the right, and a perpendicular dashed line, labeled h, descends from C to point D on segment to the right of B.
                                </p>
                                <p>
                                        Segment A D is labeled c+x and segment B D is labeled x.
                                </p>
                        </description>
                        <xi:include href="./law_of_cosines_image2.xml" />
                </image>
        </figure>
</subsection>
